/** Facts about eras, in calendar years (negative is BCE). A fact belongs to every lifetime that overlaps it. */
val ERA = listOf(
    Era(-300000, -250000, "The oldest known fossils of our species, from Jebel Irhoud in Morocco, are about 300,000 years old: right around this lifetime."),
    Era(-300000, -40000, "Neanderthals were living across Europe and western Asia. Most people outside Africa today still carry a little Neanderthal DNA."),
    Era(-300000, -30000, "Denisovans, known from a few bones and a lot of DNA, lived in Asia. Some of their genes survive in people today, especially in Oceania."),
    Era(-300000, -12000, "Everyone alive was a hunter-gatherer. There were no farms, no villages and no writing anywhere on Earth."),
    Era(-300000, -12000, "People moved with the seasons, following animals and ripening plants. A band was usually a few dozen people."),
    Era(-300000, -12000, "Everything a family owned had to be carried. A whole household could fit in a bundle on someone’s back."),
    Era(-300000, -12000, "Most people would never see more than a few hundred other humans in their whole life."),
    Era(-300000, -10000, "Fire was already ancient technology: humans and their relatives had used it for hundreds of thousands of years."),
    Era(-300000, -10000, "Flaked by hand, an obsidian blade can be sharper than a modern steel scalpel."),
    Era(-300000, -10000, "Estimates of how many humans were alive vary widely: perhaps a few hundred thousand to a few million on the whole planet."),
    Era(-300000, -11700, "This was the Pleistocene, the epoch of the ice ages. The climate swung between long cold spells and shorter warm ones."),
    Era(-300000, -3200, "Nobody alive would have known what year it was. There were no calendars, no written names and no money."),
    Era(-300000, 1800, "Most nights were lit only by fire, the Moon and the stars. The Milky Way was a bright band across every clear sky."),
    Era(-300000, 500, "Polaris wasn’t the North Star: Earth’s axis wobbles in a cycle of about 26,000 years, so the pole of the sky moves."),
    Era(-300000, -50000, "Stars drift over tens of thousands of years, so the constellations looked noticeably different from the ones you know."),
    Era(-300000, -10000, "Many children died young, which pulled average life expectancy down. Adults who made it through childhood often lived into their 50s or 60s."),
    Era(-130000, -115000, "Earth was in a warm spell, at times a little warmer than today, and hippos wallowed in rivers in what is now Britain."),
    Era(-115000, -11700, "The last ice age was under way. Ice sheets kilometres thick slowly grew over North America and northern Europe."),
    Era(-77000, -72000, "Around 74,000 years ago, the Toba supervolcano in Sumatra erupted: the largest volcanic eruption of the last two million years."),
    Era(-80000, -70000, "Engraved pieces of ochre from Blombos Cave in South Africa, about 75,000 years old, are among the earliest known abstract designs."),
    Era(-70000, -50000, "Small groups of modern humans were spreading out of Africa. Almost everyone outside Africa descends from them."),
    Era(-65000, -45000, "People reached Australia by around 50,000 to 65,000 years ago, crossing open sea: some of the earliest evidence of seafaring."),
    Era(-47000, -43000, "Some of the oldest known cave paintings, in Sulawesi, Indonesia, were made at least 45,000 years ago."),
    Era(-43000, -38000, "Neanderthals disappeared around 40,000 years ago, after sharing Europe with modern humans for thousands of years."),
    Era(-42000, -35000, "Flutes carved from bird bone and mammoth ivory, found in Germany, show people were making music about 40,000 years ago."),
    Era(-41000, -35000, "The Lion Man, carved from mammoth ivory in what is now Germany, is about 40,000 years old: one of the oldest known figurative sculptures."),
    Era(-37000, -33000, "The lions, horses and rhinos painted in Chauvet Cave in France were made around 36,000 years ago."),
    Era(-50000, -10000, "Woolly mammoths, woolly rhinos, cave bears and giant deer roamed the cold grasslands of Europe and Asia."),
    Era(-40000, -10000, "Bone needles with eyes let people sew fitted clothing, a big help in ice-age winters."),
    Era(-30000, -12000, "Wolves were becoming dogs, the first animals humans ever domesticated, at least 15,000 years ago."),
    Era(-26000, -19000, "This was the Last Glacial Maximum: the ice sheets were at their largest and the sea was about 120 metres lower than today."),
    Era(-26000, -12000, "With the sea so low, you could walk from Siberia to Alaska on dry land."),
    Era(-16000, -6500, "Doggerland, a now-drowned country of hills, rivers and marshes, joined Britain to mainland Europe. People lived there."),
    Era(-20000, -15000, "In China, people were already making clay cooking pots, around 20,000 years ago: thousands of years before farming."),
    Era(-19000, -15000, "The paintings in the cave of Lascaux, in France, were made about 17,000 years ago."),
    Era(-16000, -13000, "People were living in the Americas by at least 15,000 years ago, and possibly much earlier."),
    Era(-13000, -9600, "The ice age was ending. The world warmed, sea levels rose by tens of metres and forests spread north."),
    Era(-9600, -8000, "Göbekli Tepe, in today’s Türkiye, was built around 9600 BCE: huge carved stone pillars raised by hunter-gatherers, 6,000 years before Stonehenge."),
    Era(-10000, -8000, "Farming was beginning in the Fertile Crescent: wheat, barley and lentils, and soon sheep and goats."),
    Era(-9000, -7000, "Jericho had a stone wall and tower around 8000 BCE, making it one of the oldest known towns."),
    Era(-7500, -5700, "Çatalhöyük, a town of several thousand people in Anatolia, had no streets: people walked across the roofs and climbed in through the top."),
    Era(-8000, -5000, "Rice was being domesticated in China, and maize was being bred from a wild grass called teosinte in Mexico."),
    Era(-6600, -6000, "Rising seas flooded the last land bridge, and Britain became an island."),
    Era(-10000, -3000, "Early farmers were often shorter and less healthy than the hunter-gatherers before them: more grain, less variety, more disease."),
    Era(-8000, 1800, "Most people farmed, and most never travelled more than a day’s walk from where they were born."),
    Era(-10000, 1492, "Potatoes, tomatoes, chocolate and chilli peppers were unknown outside the Americas."),
    Era(-300000, 1400, "Nobody anywhere had ever drunk coffee: it became a drink in the 15th century, in Yemen."),
    Era(-5000, -3500, "People were learning to smelt copper from ore: the start of the age of metals."),
    Era(-3600, -3200, "The wheel appears around 3500 BCE: after pottery, farming and sailing boats."),
    Era(-3350, -3250, "Ötzi the Iceman lived and died in the Alps around 3300 BCE. His frozen body, found in 1991, has 61 tattoos."),
    Era(-3400, -3000, "Writing was invented in Mesopotamia around 3200 BCE, at first mostly to keep accounts of grain and goats."),
    Era(-3000, -2000, "Stonehenge was being built in stages, starting around 3000 BCE."),
